WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:14.519
There have been numerous world-renowned prison
escapes spanning across the decades, but let's

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:20.390
take a look at the most ingenious ones, the
escapes that took the most planning, dedication

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.890
and outright bravery.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.380
Choi Gap-bok

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:30.969
South Korean prisoner Choi Gap-bok escaped
by squeezing himself through the food slot

00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:35.570
of his cell door, you know, the small slot
on every prison door?

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:41.850
Yes that's right, he actually contorted his
whole body through a hole only 15 centimetres

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.960
(6 inches) tall and 43 centimetres (17 inches)
wide.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.999
Choi Gap-bok was 50 and in prison for burglary.

00:00:47.999 --> 00:00:54.070
He had spent most of his life in prison and
regularly practiced yoga, which must have

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.670
aided his escape.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:01.190
One night, before lights out, he asked the
prison guard for a special skin ointment,

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.289
which they gave to him.

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:06.880
I'm sure the guards didn't think he would
be using it to squeeze through a prison door.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.820
Choi Gap-bok was only 5-foot-4-inches which
helped, but it's still a bloody impressive

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.670
feat no matter your size.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.220
He squeezed his entire body through the hole
in only 30 seconds.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.390
Unfortunately he was caught again only 6 days
later, and put into a cell with a smaller

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.600
food slot.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.600
Frank Abagnale

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:35.040
Frank Abagnale, the infamous conman that Leo
DiCaprio played in the film "Catch Me If You

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.280
Can", had successfully impersonated pilots,
teachers, doctors and lawyers.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.800
So blagging his way out of prison was nothing
short of a day's work.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.980
Frank was imprisoned for 12 years for fraud.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:55.430
He got a stroke of luck, when in 1971, the
U.S. marshal transporting him forgot his detention

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.230
commitment papers.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:03.170
Frank took this opportunity to subtly drop
the hint to one of the guards that he might

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:09.259
actually be an undercover prison inspector,
disguised as an inmate, so the guards gave

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:11.230
him preferential treatment.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:17.590
He then called his friend Jean Sebring, who
had been previously visited by the FBI agent

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.640
in charge of Frank's case.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.640
The FBI agent, Joe Shea, had left his business
card with Jean.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.500
Jean then pretended to be a magazine writer
reporting on prisons, to also get the business

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.760
card of a prison inspector.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.340
She visited Frank Abagnale in prison, posing
as his fiancé and slipped him both business

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.569
cards.

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:43.391
Frank used the prison inspector's business
card to prove to his guards, that he was in

00:02:43.391 --> 00:02:45.700
fact an undercover inspector.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.770
He told the guards it was imperative he speak
to the FBI immediately.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:56.810
Frank handed them the FBI agent's business
card, but of course Frank's friend Jean Sebring

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:01.690
had already doctored the card so it displayed
her number instead.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:04.690
Jean picked up the phone, posing as the FBI.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:09.360
Jean said she needed to meet with Frank right
outside the prison immediately.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.030
The guards happily obliged, after all, they
didn't want to get on the wrong side of a

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.959
prison inspector and FBI agent.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:22.240
The guards watched as Frank Abagnale walked
right out of the prison, to be collected by

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.470
Jean, and they both rode off into the sunset,
guilty as hell.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.810
John Dillinger

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:34.390
John Dillinger had already escaped from another
prison in 1933 using smuggled in rifles.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:40.970
So in 1934 when he was arrested again for
a bank heist he was placed in the Lake County

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.440
Jail, which was known as "escape-proof".

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.020
The jail was constantly guarded by an army
of policemen and National Guard.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:56.050
Yet Dillinger still managed a daring escape,
by fashioning a bar of soap to look like a

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:57.050
gun.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:02.870
He forced his way out of jail with his "soap-gun"
and cheekily stole the sheriff's Ford to make

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:03.870
his getaway.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.910
But he was later caught by the FBI.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.050
The Great Escape

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:13.819
No prison escape in history has every come
close to the complexity of "The Great Escape".

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:20.650
The escape was from Stalag Luft III, a German
prison in operation during World War II.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:27.680
It took about one year and the efforts of
600 prisoners to dig 3 tunnels, which they

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.310
nicknamed "Tom", "Dick" and "Harry".

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:35.210
The tunnels were 30 feet deep and tunnelled
underneath the the main fence.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:40.130
The tunnels required complex systems such
as wooden blocks for support, a series of

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:45.600
lamps and electrical wire, an air pump to
supply air to the prisoners who were digging

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.030
and a rail cart system to move the earth quicker.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:55.740
The tunnels should of surfaced in a nearby
forest but they dug them a little short and

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:01.850
during the actual escape they surfaced in
plain view of the guards, consequently only

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.180
76 prisoners escaped.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.470
All but 3 of the escapees where eventually
caught by the Nazis.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:09.470
Colditz

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:15.850
The infamous World War II, German prisoner
of war camp, Colditz, was a castle situated

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.460
on a cliff overlooking the town of Colditz.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:24.180
There were many attempted escapes from Colditz,
but the best planned of them all occurred

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:31.550
when two British pilots, Bill Goldfinch and
Jack Best planned to construct a two-man glider.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:37.810
Jack and Bill constructed the glider, bit
by bit in the lower attic above the chapel.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.370
The plan was to ride the glider from the roof
across the river Mulde.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.710
The pair built a secret wall in the attic
to conceal the glider.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:52.430
The glider was painstakingly constructed from
stolen pieces of wood such as bed slats and

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:53.750
floor boards.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.800
The germans who ran the camp focused their
searches on tunnels, it never struck them

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.400
to search the attic for secret glider workshops.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:09.370
The war ended and the camp was relieved by
the allies before Jack and Bill had a chance

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:10.930
to make their escape.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.840
However Channel 4 made a replica of the glider
in a documentary about the escape called "Escape

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:17.900
from Colditz".

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:27.150
In the documentary the glider was flown successfully,
showing the escape would of likely been successful.

